{{Quote|Take this road to the left and it'll lead you straight to Merchant Row. Once you see the big statue of a Jedi priestess, you'll know you're there.|[[Salju]] to Izal Garsea|<ref name="A Crash of Fate" />}}
A [[human]]<ref name="ROTS" /> [[Gender|female]] individual was at one point a '''Jedi priestess'''.<ref name="A Crash of Fate" /> A giant statue of the [[Jedi]] was located in<ref name="ROTS" /> the [[Great Hall (Jedi Temple)|Great Hall]]<ref name="Jedi's Duty">{{StoryCite|story=A Jedi's Duty|book=Stories of Jedi and Sith}}</ref> at the entrance of the [[Imperial Palace|Jedi Temple]] on [[Coruscant]]. It held a [[book]] in it's left hand and a ball was permanently floating above it's outstretched right [[Limb|arm]].<ref name="ROTS" /> A second statue also stood in another similar hall in the Temple.<ref name="Part I">{{Kenobi|Part I}}</ref> A much smaller and patinated version of the statue of the same design<ref name="ArtofGE" /> also stood in [[Black Spire Outpost]] on [[Batuu]] next to the entrance of the [[Dok-Ondar's Den of Antiquities|Den of Antiquities]] of the [[Ithorian]] merchant [[Dok-Ondar]],<ref name="GE">{{GEAttr|Dok-Ondar's Den of Antiquities}}</ref> who was an avid collector of Jedi and [[Sith]] artifacts.<ref name="GE3">[[Galaxy's Edge 3|''Galaxy's Edge'' 3]]</ref> This version was mostly identical to the one at the Temple, but had the ball directly attached to it's finger tips.<ref name="GE" /> [[Izal Garsea]] thought that the statue looked severe and reverent in ways she had never learned to be.<ref name="A Crash of Fate" />

{{Char-stub}}

==Behind the scenes==
The statue of the individual first appeared in the [[2005]] film [[Star Wars: Episode III Revenge of the Sith|''Star Wars'': Episode III ''Revenge of the Sith'']].<ref name="ROTS" /> It was envisioned by concept artist [[Ryan Church]] to fill the empty space of the animatics of the Great Hall, on which [[Eric Carney]] and director [[George Lucas]] were currently working on. Afterwards it's the design was refined by [[Aaron McBride]]<ref name="Art of ROTS">''[[The Art of Revenge of the Sith]]''</ref>&mdash;with concept art dated to March 12, [[2004]]&mdash;and a painted sculpture was done by [[Industrial Light & Magic]] model shop sculptor [[Richard Miller]].<ref name="McBride IG">{{Instagram|username=amcb_toraidhe|name=[[Aaron McBride]]|url=p/BzD3hDSD5w1|quote=Statue concept I did for the Jedi Temple atrium in #StarWars #RevengeoftheSith (2005) From l-r: Concept art, final painted sculpture done by legendary @ILMVFX Model Shop sculptor Richard Miller & in the finished film.|nolive=1|nobackup=1}} {{C|Post mirrored by {{Twitter|username=PhilSzostak|name=[[Phil Szostak]]|url=status/1142851521219948550|date=2019-07-23|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/YMzaF}}}}</ref> A replica of the statue stands in front of the gift shop Dok-Ondar's Den of Antiquities at [[Star Wars: Galaxy's Edge|''Star Wars'': Galaxy's Edge]] at at both [[Disneyland]] and [[Disney's Hollywood Studios]],<ref name="GE" /> which first opened on [[May 31]], [[2019]].<ref name="D23">{{D23|url=a-to-z/star-wars-galaxys-edge|text=Disney A to Z: Star Wars: Galaxy's Edge}}</ref> The 2019 spin-off novel ''[[A Crash of Fate]]'' by [[Zoraida Córdova]] then identified the individual as a Jedi priestess.<ref name="A Crash of Fate" /> The statue is also mentioned and identified as a Jedi priestess in the [[2021]] art book ''[[The Art of Star Wars: Galaxy's Edge]]''. It is however absent in concept art of the Den of Antiquities, although a robed and hooded statue appears in one of the images.<ref name="ArtofGE" />

